<ContentLayout structuredData={__sd} title="Why your USB-C cable is charging slow (and how to tell) | StackGeist" description="Slow USB-C charging is commonly caused by the charger, cable, port, dock, or device negotiating less power than expected. Diagnose which side is limiting the wattage before you replace anything." active="guides">
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / USB-C cable charging slow</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Connectivity troubleshooting</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Why your USB-C cable is charging slow (and how to tell).</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">A slow-charging USB-C setup has three suspects: the cable, the charger, and the device. Each one can silently negotiate down to a lower profile and each one hides the reason. Diagnose which side is the ceiling before buying anything new.</p>
    <div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Swap in a rated 100W or 240W USB-C to USB-C cable. If speed jumps, the old cable was under-spec. If nothing changes, check whether a second device is sharing the charger, then confirm the device's own maximum draw. Most "slow" charging is expected charging measured against the wrong ceiling.</p></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Check the cable's rated wattage first.</h2><p>For USB-C to USB-C power, a 3A cable supports up to 60W at 20V. More than 60W requires a 5A electronically marked cable. USB-IF's current certified-cable markings use 60W or 240W power labels; older 100W products still exist in the market, so verify the manufacturer's documented rating rather than judging by appearance.[1]</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Look for '60W', '100W', '5A', or '240W' printed on the cable or listed on the box.</li><li>Do not infer the power rating from thickness alone. If the rating is unknown, treat the cable as unverified and test with a known-rated cable.</li><li>Length can affect electrical and signal-performance requirements, so stay within the device or cable manufacturer's supported length for the intended mode.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Confirm the cable actually has an e-marker.</h2><p>Five-amp USB-C cables use electronic marking to advertise cable capabilities used during USB Power Delivery negotiation. For charging above 60W, use a cable whose manufacturer explicitly documents the required 5A/high-power rating rather than trying to infer it from appearance.</p><div class="note">Power rating and data rate are separate. A cable can support high charging power while carrying only USB 2.0 data, or support high-speed data with a lower power rating. Read both specifications.</div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Test with a known full-featured cable.</h2><p>Borrow or buy a cable you can identify — a rated 100W or 240W USB-C to USB-C, ideally the one that shipped with the charger. If wattage jumps to what you expect, the original cable was the limit and can be replaced or reserved for phone-only duty. If nothing changes, the charger or device is the ceiling.</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Verify the charger's per-port output, not the total.</h2><p>Multi-port chargers redistribute their available power according to a model-specific port-allocation table. A charger advertised at 100W may provide less than 100W to the laptop port when other outputs are active. Read the charger's printed output combinations or official manual.</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong>One port active</strong><p>Use the port the manufacturer identifies for maximum single-device output.</p></div><div><strong>Multiple ports active</strong><p>Output may be redistributed. Check the exact port-allocation table for the charger.</p></div><div><strong>Result still low</strong><p>Move on to cable, device-input, dock, and thermal checks instead of assuming the charger label is wrong.</p></div></div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Check the device's own maximum draw.</h2><p>A charger and cable can deliver only a power mode the device supports and requests. Laptop charging limits vary widely even within the same product family, and some models support faster charging only with specific adapters or USB-C PD profiles. Check the exact model's official power specification before judging the charger.[2]</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 06</p><h2>Rule out heat and dirty ports.</h2><p>Chargers can reduce output when thermal protection engages, and debris can prevent a USB-C plug from seating correctly. Let an overheated charger cool, power the computer down before inspection, and follow the device manufacturer's cleaning guidance.</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Move the charger off the laptop and off the carpet.</li><li>Inspect the USB-C port on both ends under a bright light.</li><li>Use compressed air if recommended by the manufacturer; do not insert metal or improvised tools into the connector.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>When to replace the cable versus everything else.</h2><p>If Step 03 restored the expected charging rate, the cable was the limiting part; replace it with a cable explicitly rated for the power level your laptop and charger negotiate. If Step 04 revealed a per-port drop, moving the laptop to a solo charger is cheaper than a new brick. If the device's own ceiling is the limit, no cable or charger will make it faster.</p><div class="cards">
        <a class="card" href="/gear/budget-tech/iniu-usb-c-to-usb-c-cable-240w-6-6ft"><h3>INIU 240W 6.6ft USB-C cable</h3><p>A rated 240W cable with e-marker, USB 2.0 data, that covers every current USB-PD profile.</p></a>
        <a class="card" href="/gear/budget-tech/500w-multi-port-gan-charging-station"><h3>500W multi-port GaN station</h3><p>When one charger has to feed a laptop, a phone, a tablet, and a spare port at once.</p></a>
        <a class="card" href="/guides/usb-c-cable-buying-guide"><h3>USB-C cable buying guide</h3><p>What the wattage and speed markings actually mean, and how to identify an unlabeled cable.</p></a>
      </div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Related fixes</p><h2>Keep troubleshooting the same system.</h2><div class="cards"><a class="card" href="/guides/usb-c-charging-display-problems"><h3>USB-C charging and display hub</h3><p>Work through the full charging path.</p></a><a class="card" href="/guides/gan-charger-not-charging-laptop-full-speed"><h3>GaN charger not charging full speed</h3><p>Check PD negotiation and shared wattage.</p></a><a class="card" href="/guides/laptop-says-slow-charger-with-100w-usb-c-charger/"><h3>100W charger but Windows says slow charger</h3><p>Diagnose per-port output, cable limits, port choice, and dock losses.</p></a></div></section>
